---
alwaysApply: true
---

You are working on a production-grade multi-tenant SaaS built with:
- Next.js App Router
- Route Handlers (src/app/api/**/route.ts)
- NextAuth (JWT strategy)
- Prisma + PostgreSQL
- Zod for validation
- Tailwind CSS and shadcn/ui
- Server-first architecture

Follow these rules strictly.

────────────────────────────────────────
ARCHITECTURE
────────────────────────────────────────

1) App Router only
- Use Next.js App Router conventions exclusively.
- API routes must be implemented as Route Handlers under:
  src/app/api/**/route.ts
- Do NOT introduce pages/, pages/api/, or legacy patterns.

2) Server-first rendering
- All components are Server Components by default.
- Add `"use client"` ONLY when strictly required:
  - React hooks (useState, useEffect, etc.)
  - Browser-only APIs
  - Event handlers (onClick, onChange, etc.)
- Keep client components as small leaf components to minimize bundle size.

3) No Server Actions
- Do NOT create or use Server Actions (`"use server"`).
- All mutations and side effects must go through API Route Handlers.
- If asked to use Server Actions, propose a Route Handler instead.

4) Route groups must be respected
- Respect existing route groups:
  (public), (auth), (product)
- Do not create new groups or routes without following existing structure.
- Do not invent routing patterns.

────────────────────────────────────────
SECURITY & MULTI-TENANCY
────────────────────────────────────────

5) Never trust the client
- All security enforcement happens on the server.
- Client-side checks are UX-only and never authoritative.

6) Auth is mandatory
- Any route under /app/* requires authentication.
- Any API under /api/* (except /api/auth/*) requires authentication.
- Always validate the session/token inside the endpoint.

7) Tenant scoping is mandatory
- All tenant-scoped data MUST be filtered by tenantId/workspaceId.
- Never query or mutate data without tenant isolation.
- Do NOT assume tenant context from the client.

8) Tenant resolution
- Tenant is resolved server-side using database membership.
- Prefer a single shared helper to resolve the active tenant.
- Never hardcode tenantId values.

9) Admin access
- Middleware allowlists are NOT sufficient for data access.
- Admin-only endpoints must enforce authorization inside the API handler.

────────────────────────────────────────
VALIDATION & TYPES
────────────────────────────────────────

10) Zod validation required
- All API request payloads must be validated with Zod.
- Validate req.json() before using the data.
- Return consistent error responses with proper HTTP status codes.

11) Type safety
- TypeScript strict mode is assumed.
- Avoid `any`.
- Avoid `@ts-ignore` unless absolutely necessary and documented.

────────────────────────────────────────
PRISMA & PERFORMANCE
────────────────────────────────────────

12) Prisma query discipline
- Prefer `select` over broad `include`.
- Avoid loading unnecessary relations.
- Avoid N+1 queries.
- Use batch operations and transactions wisely.

13) Transactions
- Keep interactive transactions short.
- Do NOT run heavy loops or long operations inside transactions.

14) Multi-tenant integrity
- Every Prisma query touching tenant data must enforce tenant isolation.
- Cross-tenant queries require explicit platform-level authorization.

────────────────────────────────────────
UI (TAILWIND + SHADCN)
────────────────────────────────────────

15) Component boundaries
- UI primitives live in src/components/ui/** (shadcn-style).
- Domain components live in:
  - src/components/app
  - src/components/auth
  - src/components/settings
- Do not duplicate UI primitives.

16) UI behavior
- All user-facing screens must include:
  - loading state
  - empty state
  - error state

────────────────────────────────────────
DEFINITION OF DONE
────────────────────────────────────────

17) Any change must:
- Compile successfully (npm run build)
- Respect TypeScript types
- Not introduce new architectural patterns
- Keep security, tenant isolation, and performance intact

18) When implementing features:
- Propose a plan before large changes.
- Modify only the necessary files.
- Avoid speculative or unused code.
